WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.620
My father, when he talked 
to my grandmother,

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.430
they'd have to use these signs.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.220
For example, some of the basics was like,
“yes” and “no”...

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.449
“good” and “bad.”

00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:12.667
[light, rhythmic music]

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.053
This is one of the oldest languages in 
North America.

00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:18.471
[rhythmic music persists]

00:00:18.471 --> 00:00:20.089
This is a “Crow Indian.”

00:00:20.089 --> 00:00:21.840
This was a “white person.”

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.029
It’s called 
 Plains Indian Sign Language.

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:30.490
One of many indigenous sign languages, 
 broadly known as “Hand Talk,”

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:34.970
that were once used widely 
 by both deaf and hearing people.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:36.610
It was the means for commerce.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.807
It was the means for economics.

00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:40.162
[music builds]

00:00:45.461 --> 00:00:48.260
Variations of Hand Talk 
 were so commonly used

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.180
that it would influence the formation 
of American Sign Language, or ASL,

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.130
the predominant sign language 
 used today in the US and Canada.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.993
But Hand Talk has mostly 
 been written out of history.

00:01:07.702 --> 00:01:09.928
[music fades out]

00:01:11.928 --> 00:01:18.637
[quick and rhythmic piano notes, urgent 
but persistent]

00:01:18.637 --> 00:01:24.086
When a Spanish colonizer first reached 
 one indigenous community in the southwest,

00:01:24.086 --> 00:01:27.498
he noted that indigenous 
people knew signs so well

00:01:27.498 --> 00:01:29.571
“that there was no need 
 for an interpreter.”

00:01:29.571 --> 00:01:31.478
[rhythmic piano continues]

00:01:31.478 --> 00:01:34.780
That sign language was likely 
 a variation of Hand Talk.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.847
And these journals can trace it back 
 to at least the 1500s.

00:01:38.847 --> 00:01:41.149
But if you take a look at a 
 different kind of writing,

00:01:41.149 --> 00:01:44.583
the evidence suggests it 
 goes back even further.

00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:47.446
[urgent and rhythmic music continues]

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:09.769
There’s also rock writing depicting “prayer,”
versus a similar sign for “prayer.”

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:13.842
Or a symbol for “elder”, indicating a
person with a walking stick, 

00:02:13.842 --> 00:02:16.026
versus a sign for “elder.”

00:02:16.026 --> 00:02:21.398
And this: a symbol for “hunger,” 
versus a sign for “hunger.”

00:02:21.398 --> 00:02:23.898
Hand Talk, which 
 researchers also call

00:02:23.898 --> 00:02:28.570
“North American Indian Sign Language,” 
has many regional variations,

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.580
like Northeast Indian Sign Language here,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.810
or Southwest Indian Sign Language here;

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:40.123
as well as tribe-specific variations 
 within those regions.

00:02:40.123 --> 00:02:44.327
And here — from the Gulf Coast region, up
through the Great Plains into Canada,

00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:45.345
[music stops]

00:02:45.345 --> 00:02:47.925
was the home of Plains 
 Indian Sign Language.

00:02:47.925 --> 00:02:49.225
[curious music begins]

00:02:49.225 --> 00:02:53.310
PISL is one of the most well-
documented Hand Talk variations,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.040
probably because of how 
 widely it was used.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.180
Plains Indian Sign Language was the medium
for communication of intertribal nations.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.984
And that had a lot to do with 
 how those tribes lived.

00:03:17.994 --> 00:03:21.259
By the 19th century, tens of 
 thousands of indigenous people 

00:03:21.259 --> 00:03:24.123
were reported to have known PISL.

00:03:24.123 --> 00:03:29.140
It was the standard language used 
just as English is used today at the United Nations,

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:34.297
especially in commerce, in gaining intelligence, in hunting,

00:03:34.297 --> 00:03:35.170
[music stops]

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.697
even in warfare.

00:03:36.697 --> 00:03:37.788
[urgent staccato music starts]

00:03:37.788 --> 00:03:43.295
In PISL, there’s no finger-spelled alphabet
like you find in other sign languages.

00:03:43.295 --> 00:03:46.790
Its building blocks are 
 roughly 4,000 basic words

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.340
that form its vocabulary.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.319
For the word “war,” you’d sign 
“big” and “fight.”

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:57.430
For the word “beautiful," you’d sign
“face” and “good.”

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:01.775
Or for “stream," you’d sign the words
“river” and “little”.

00:04:02.866 --> 00:04:04.024
There is...

00:04:05.564 --> 00:04:06.486
a stream...

00:04:07.668 --> 00:04:10.930
on the other side of some
bluffs over there.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:16.540
The way PISL works also shows indigenous 
ways of thinking about the world.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.260
For example, let’s take 
 the simple question:

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.630
How old are you?

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:23.125
First, there’s a single sign for “question.”

00:04:33.279 --> 00:04:35.769
So for a question about someone’s age,

00:04:35.769 --> 00:04:39.396
you’d use the motion for question
with the motion for “winter”.

00:04:42.524 --> 00:04:44.490
How many winters are you?

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.380
That's what I ask.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.924
In PISL you measure months by moons,
days by the sun.

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:54.400
And to refer to different times of day,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.888
you would show hand placement according to
the position of the sun in the sky.

00:04:58.888 --> 00:05:02.943
So this sign for morning, 
afternoon, or night.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:09.464
If you look at some of this side-by-side
with American Sign Language,

00:05:09.464 --> 00:05:11.801
you’ll notice some similarities.

00:05:11.801 --> 00:05:16.355
Like that sign for moon,
along with other similar words.

00:05:16.355 --> 00:05:20.881
Researchers believe another form of Hand
Talk, Northeast Indian Sign language,

00:05:20.881 --> 00:05:23.120
was one of the contributors to ASL.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.317
[music builds]

00:05:35.826 --> 00:05:37.503
[bass note rings and music fades]

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:41.008
[film reel static and ominous string music begins]

00:05:41.008 --> 00:05:44.220
This film from September 1930

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:49.888
shows one of the largest gatherings of
intertribal indigenous leaders ever filmed.

00:05:49.888 --> 00:05:53.220
They were brought together 
by General Hugh L. Scott

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.660
to document and preserve PISL.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:56.615
[music fades]

00:05:56.615 --> 00:05:59.523
“I have brought you from every direction
to sit in this council."

00:06:00.796 --> 00:06:06.659
"I have come myself from
very far in the east to sit with you."

00:06:06.659 --> 00:06:11.803
"Young men are not learning your sign language,
and soon it will disappear from this country.”

00:06:11.803 --> 00:06:13.789
[music holds an ominous tone and fades]

00:06:15.335 --> 00:06:18.039
By the time this gathering took place,

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:20.552
Scott’s predictions were
already coming true.

00:06:20.552 --> 00:06:22.370
[slow, somber music begins]

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:25.830
In the late 1800s, the US government 
began sending

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:29.139
Native children to government 
residential schools,

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:33.720
where administrators enforced a strict 
“English-only” policy

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.840
explicitly designed to cut off
children from their families,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.940
their culture, and their native identities.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:45.430
They were punished severely for
speaking their languages.

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:47.460
And that also meant signing.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:51.370
And if they signed, sometimes
many of these kids were beaten,

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:54.153
and they were systematically...

00:06:55.798 --> 00:06:57.598
psychologically destroyed.

00:07:22.524 --> 00:07:25.762
In the violent pursuit of
standardizing language in the US,

00:07:25.762 --> 00:07:26.870
[music fades to silence]

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.345
we lost tens of thousands
of indigenous signers.

00:07:30.345 --> 00:07:34.506
There are only a few dozen
fluent PISL signers left.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:36.743
 [piano notes start to play]

00:07:36.743 --> 00:07:41.120
Today, many indigenous
deaf Americans use ASL.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.559
But many of ASL's signs are rooted
in a cultural experience that isn't theirs.

00:08:10.087 --> 00:08:13.396
Even though ASL has an extensive vocabulary,

00:08:13.396 --> 00:08:16.796
there’s one big thing it doesn’t have
signs for:

00:08:16.796 --> 00:08:18.833
names of tribes.

00:08:18.833 --> 00:08:21.753
In ASL, you'd have to
spell out tribe names.

00:08:21.753 --> 00:08:26.460
But with PISL, there’s a sign
for every Native American tribe,

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.240
with gestures connected
to their meanings.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.335
For example, this sign for Crow
imitates a knotted hairstyle.

00:08:33.335 --> 00:08:34.590
[music fades out]

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:37.877
This sign for Shoshoni imitates
the movement of a snake.

00:08:37.877 --> 00:08:41.020
And this sign for Cheyenne
shows a striped arrow.

00:08:42.475 --> 00:08:45.100
That’s why Melanie and
other Deaf native people

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:49.390
are beginning to use
a mix of PISL and ASL.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.723
[rhythm double times, becoming more urgent]

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.909
Okay, Hand Talking.

00:09:22.819 --> 00:09:26.340
Plains Indian Sign Language
is still endangered.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:31.427
But many indigenous people are working
to incorporate it more into everyday life.

00:09:31.427 --> 00:09:32.355
[hopeful music builds]

00:09:32.355 --> 00:09:36.806
And pushing for it to be included
in more mainstream education.

00:09:36.806 --> 00:09:42.127
Because as a language,
PISL is more than words.

00:09:42.127 --> 00:09:44.790
It carries their history.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:48.100
We not only can hear a native language.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:49.750
We can do it.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:51.500
We can see it.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.440
And we can also feel it.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.830
Plains Indian Sign Language
is almost in our DNA.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.263
[first song of curious, driving tones plays]

00:10:09.445 --> 00:10:11.197
[music fades to silent]

